# M5 review (reviewer K): docs/design.md's "A thread never observes another
# thread's x87 or SSE state" names a scenario, fp_isolation, that M5 did not
# add; test=ring3 switches FP state by hand only. Here the scheduler does it
# (user/root/src/isolation.rs). The root task gives threads X and Y, at
# priority 100, FS and GS bases of their own (TCB_WriteRegisters words 18
# and 19), loads x87 and SSE values of its own and resumes them. Each
# thread loads XMM0 to XMM15, ST0, ST1, MXCSR and FCW with its own values
# and for 80 ms of TSC time stores and compares them, with fs:[0] and
# gs:[0] against its markers; then for 80 ms holds rbx, rbp, rcx, rsi and
# r8 to r15, DF and AC in a loop that compares them on every pass; then
# checks its FP and segment state again. A shared "last thread to run" word
# counts the times the other thread ran in between, which only the timer's
# preemption allows. The root passes if every check held, each thread saw
# the other run at least twice in each phase, and its own x87 and SSE state
# survived both threads.
cmdline: test=isolation
exit: success
expect: microL4 kernel
expect: cmdline: "test=isolation"
expect: root: microL4 root task
expect: root: cmdline "test=isolation"
expect: isolation: threads X and Y at priority 100
expect: isolation: thread X: x87, SSE and segment check 0, general-register check 0;
expect: isolation: thread Y: x87, SSE and segment check 0, general-register check 0;
expect: isolation: the root task's x87 and SSE state: check 0
expect: TEST PASS
expect: DEBUG HALT 0 from TCB
